\use{document}
\use{chem}

\title{Reactions with \texttt{chem}}
\author{The texish project}
\maketitle

\section{Reaction equations}

A reaction is a row of species joined by \texttt{+} and a reaction arrow. Atom
counts are subscripts (\texttt{\\dn}) and ionic charges superscripts
(\texttt{\\up}); a leading number is the stoichiometric coefficient, at full size.
The reaction arrow is the math symbol \texttt{\$\\longrightarrow\$}, a real glyph
from the math font. The combustion of methane:

\bigskip
\centerline{\font lmroman 14 regular CH\dn{4} + 2O\dn{2} $\longrightarrow$ CO\dn{2} + 2H\dn{2}O}
\bigskip

\noindent A reversible reaction uses the equilibrium arrow
\texttt{\$\\rightleftharpoons\$} — the Haber process:

\bigskip
\centerline{\font lmroman 14 regular N\dn{2} + 3H\dn{2} $\rightleftharpoons$ 2NH\dn{3}}
\bigskip

\noindent And a dissociation, showing ionic charges with \texttt{\\up}:

\bigskip
\centerline{\font lmroman 14 regular NaCl $\longrightarrow$ Na\up{+} + Cl\up{-}}
\bigskip

\noindent Because each species is just an inline box, a drawn \texttt{\\picture}
structure can stand in place of a formula and the same \texttt{+} and arrow lay
out the equation.

\vfill\eject
